// Macro-expansion diagnostic codes.
//
// Written in subset 1 (meta/SUBSET_1.md §4). Cycle 0.6.1.
//
// Centralised for the reason 0.1.6, 0.2.1, 0.3.1, 0.4.1 and 0.5.1 give: a mistyped
// code literal at a call site silently invents a NEW code, and the test asserting
// the old one then fails for a reason unrelated to the bug it guards.

// An invocation naming no macro. Its own code rather than the resolver's
// "unknown name", because `#name(…)` is also how every compiler builtin is spelled
// (D-046) — a reader who sees this wrote `#` and meant a macro.
mod:macro_codes;
pub func:MACRO_UNKNOWN = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-001"; };

// An invocation passing the wrong number of arguments.
pub func:MACRO_ARITY = string() never fails        { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-002"; };

// ONE INVOCATION NESTED TOO DEEPLY. A program that is too complicated.
pub func:MACRO_TOO_DEEP = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-003"; };

// THE FIXED-POINT LOOP DID NOT SETTLE. A program that does not terminate — two
// macros expanding into each other, or one into itself.
//
// ITS OWN CODE, not shared with the depth bound. They are different mistakes with
// unrelated fixes, and a single "expansion limit exceeded" would make the second
// look like the first.
//
// DECIDED IN TWO PLACES since 1.6.1e step 3 (DEF-172, DEF-173). A macro that
// reaches itself through the invocations the bodies write is refused at its
// DECLARATION, before a round is run, with the chain in the sentence; what the
// round bound still stops is a chain that is finite and too long, refused at the
// invocation left standing after the last round.
pub func:MACRO_NO_FIXPOINT = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-004"; };

// A body spliced where what it emits does not fit — fields into something that is
// not a struct, declarations into an expression. And, since 1.6.1e step 3, the two
// bodies that fit NO site: one that declares a `macro:` (macros are collected
// before expansion, so an emitted one could never be invoked), and a range standing
// as a whole value pattern (a pattern's kind is the parser's).
pub func:MACRO_WRONG_SHAPE = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-005"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER: a node kind the clone has no case for. `RESOLVE_INTERNAL`
// and `TYPE_INTERNAL` exist for the same situation — a reader who sees it has found
// a hole in the expander and there is nothing they can change in their source.
pub func:MACRO_INTERNAL = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-006"; };

// A MACRO NAMED FROM OUTSIDE THE MODULE THAT DECLARES IT (D-124). Its own code
// rather than `MACRO_UNKNOWN`, because the two are unrelated mistakes: one is a
// name nobody wrote, the other is a name written somewhere it cannot be reached,
// and only the second has an obvious fix.
pub func:MACRO_NOT_LOCAL = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-007"; };

// `#caller(NAME)` WRITTEN IN A FORM THAT IS NOT ONE. It takes exactly one
// argument, that argument is a bare identifier, and it is legal only inside a
// macro body — an escape hatch with no rule is the one path that is worse than no
// escape hatch (D-057).
pub func:MACRO_CALLER_FORM = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-008"; };

// NOT A FAILURE — a note attached to one. A diagnostic whose span falls inside a
// macro body is about code the reader may not have written, and the line that made
// it wrong is the invocation, which appears nowhere in the diagnostic itself.
//
// Its own code because a note is filtered like anything else, and because a reader
// who sees it is being told WHERE to look rather than WHAT is wrong.
pub func:MACRO_EXPANDED_HERE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-009"; };
// A MACRO BODY IS DECLARATIONS OR STATEMENTS, NOT BOTH (DEF-150's c1, 1.6.1e step 2;
// MACRO_REFERENCE §3): a body that mixes the two kinds fits no site, and every reader
// of its window reads each item by the body's one kind -- a statement in a
// declarations window was a compiler trap.
pub func:MACRO_MIXED_BODY = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-010"; };

// A PARAMETER WRITTEN WHERE AN ARGUMENT CANNOT LAND (DEF-189, 1.6.1e step 3a;
// MACRO_REFERENCE §3). An argument is an expression, so a parameter's name
// standing where a TYPE'S NAME stands is never substituted, and a parameter's
// name that the body DECLARES AND WRITES AS AN EXPRESSION is a declaration every
// use of which is the argument instead. Each was accepted, in silence, with the
// argument dropped or the declaration unread. Refused at the macro's declaration,
// whether or not anything invokes it. (A name the body declares and never uses
// is MACRO_REFERENCE §10's open question, S-123, and is not refused.) Its own code rather than `MACRO_WRONG_SHAPE`: that one is
// about what a body emits and where it lands, and this is about the body's own
// names -- unrelated mistakes with unrelated fixes.
pub func:MACRO_PARAM_FORM = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MACRO-011"; };
